from data_new import X,t,CW
X("ketamine","Ketamine","diss","Dissociative anesthetic (NMDA receptor antagonist).","Ketalar; street: K, special K, ket, kitty.",
"Synthesized by Calvin Stevens at Parke-Davis in 1962 as a safer replacement for PCP; first tested in humans in 1964, FDA-approved in 1970, and used in the Vietnam War. Recreational use spread in the 1970s and in 1990s club scenes.",
"An anesthetic that largely preserves breathing, used in emergencies, children and veterinary medicine; now also used off-label in clinics for depression and pain.","Used in club and festival settings for dissociation and the 'K-hole'. Frequent use leads to bladder damage.",
"US Schedule III (1999). UK Class B. Not controlled under the UN conventions; the WHO has recommended against international control because of its medical importance.",
["Ketamine's character is floating detachment: the body feels rubbery or distant, sounds echo, and the room seems to slide. Thought becomes dreamlike and slow.","At higher levels people enter the 'K-hole', near-total disconnection where the body seems to vanish; some report out-of-body or near-death experiences, and they cannot move or speak.","Nausea, unsteadiness and loss of pain sensation make falls and injuries common, and the bladder damage from regular use (ketamine uropathy) can be severe and permanent."],
["Snorted, ketamine takes effect within about 5 to 15 minutes: numbness, a light 'drunk' feeling, and a shifting visual field.","Within minutes the body starts to feel distant; walking becomes unsteady.","The peak, about 15 to 30 minutes in, is the strongest dissociation; at high levels, the K-hole.","Effects plateau for 30 to 60 minutes.","People 'come back' over another hour, often confused and nauseated.","Tiredness and a dreamy afterglow; some feel low.","Next-day fatigue; with frequent use, bladder pain and urgency."],
{"intranasal":t("5-15 min","10-20 min","15-30 min","30-60 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","next day"),"injected":t("seconds-5 min","1-5 min","5-20 min","20-40 min","about 1 h","2-3 h","next day"),"oral":t("15-30 min","30-60 min","about 1 h","1-2 h","2-4 h","4-6 h","next day")},
{"intranasal":"Snorted powder.","injected":"Medical IV or IM; also injected recreationally.","oral":"Swallowed; some clinics use lozenges."},
"Ketamine alone rarely stops breathing, but with alcohol, opioids or benzodiazepines it can; vomiting while unresponsive and accidents are major causes of death.",
"Medical ketamine is a clear liquid in vials. Street ketamine is a white crystalline powder."+CW,"Ketamine blocks NMDA receptors and is metabolized by the liver to norketamine.",
lohi="The gap between 'floaty' and a K-hole is small, and mixing with depressants can stop breathing.",src=["Domino EF. Taming the ketamine tiger. Anesthesiology 2010","WHO Expert Committee on Drug Dependence: ketamine reviews"])

X("esketamine","Esketamine (Spravato)","diss","The S-enantiomer of ketamine, given as a nasal spray for depression.","Spravato.",
"Approved by the FDA in 2019 for treatment-resistant depression and in 2020 for depression with suicidal thoughts.","Given only in certified clinics under a restricted program (REMS), with monitoring for at least two hours afterward.","Not dispensed for home use, which limits misuse.","US Schedule III.",
["Patients describe mild-to-moderate dissociation: floaty, distant, with altered colors and sounds, starting soon after the spray.","Blood pressure rises, and nausea and dizziness are common, which is why patients are observed.","Patients must not drive until the next day after a restful sleep."],
["Effects start within minutes of the spray.","Dissociation builds over 15 to 30 minutes.","The peak is about 30 to 40 minutes.","Effects ease within about an hour.","Most effects resolve within two hours.","Tiredness.","No driving until the next day."],
{"intranasal":t("minutes","10-20 min","30-40 min","40-60 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","next day")},{"intranasal":"Nasal spray device, used only in clinics."},
"Under monitoring, serious harm is rare; risks include sedation, high blood pressure and dissociation.",
"Spravato is a single-use nasal spray device kept at the clinic.","Esketamine is the stronger mirror-image form of ketamine.",src=["DailyMed: Spravato label"])

X("pcp","PCP (phencyclidine)","diss","Dissociative anesthetic (NMDA antagonist).","Sernyl (former brand); street: angel dust, wet, sherm, fry, 'embalming fluid' cigarettes.",
"Developed by Parke-Davis in the 1950s as the anesthetic Sernyl and withdrawn from human use in 1965 because patients woke with severe agitation and delirium. Recreational use rose in the late 1960s and 1970s.",
"None in humans today; formerly a veterinary anesthetic.","PCP-dipped cigarettes remain in some US cities.","US Schedule II; UN 1971 Convention Schedule II.",
["PCP's character is numb, distorted detachment combined with stimulation: people may feel invulnerable while their sense of body and surroundings is warped.","It can cause agitation, paranoia, aggression and a psychosis resembling schizophrenia, and people often do not feel pain, so injuries are common.","High blood pressure, high body temperature, muscle breakdown and seizures can occur; effects can come and go in waves over a day or more."],
["Smoked, effects begin within minutes: numbness, unsteadiness and staring.","Detachment and blunted pain build.","The peak brings intense distortion and possible agitation or catatonia.","Effects commonly persist for 4 to 6 hours.","Comedown is long and uneven, sometimes over a day or more.","Confusion, memory gaps, low mood.","Lingering effects can last days."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("2-5 min","5-30 min","15-60 min","4-6 h","6-24 h","1-2 days","days")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Smoked, typically on dipped cigarettes or plant material."},
"PCP poisoning causes agitation, very high temperature, seizures, coma and death, as well as deaths from injuries and drowning.",
"Pure PCP is a white powder; street PCP is often a liquid with a chemical smell."+CW,"PCP blocks NMDA receptors and is long-acting.",
lohi="Effects are unpredictable at any amount; higher levels bring catatonia, seizures and dangerous agitation.",src=["NIDA: Hallucinogens DrugFacts","Bey T, Patel A. Phencyclidine intoxication and adverse effects. Cal J Emerg Med 2007"])

X("dxm","DXM (dextromethorphan)","diss","Cough suppressant that acts as a dissociative far above medical use.","Robitussin, Delsym, Coricidin HBP Cough and Cold, NyQuil, Mucinex DM; Nuedexta (with quinidine); Auvelity (with bupropion). Street: robo, triple C, skittles, dex.",
"Approved in the US in 1958 as a non-opioid cough suppressant. 'Robo-tripping' spread among teens in the 1990s and 2000s.",
"Cough suppression; with quinidine for pseudobulbar affect; with bupropion for depression since 2022.","Teens misuse cough products because they are cheap and available; many US states restrict sales to minors.","Over-the-counter in the US; not federally controlled.",
["DXM's character changes with amount, described by users as 'plateaus': from a drunk, music-enhancing buzz to strong dissociation and loss of body awareness.","It feels 'robotic', with stiff walking, sweating, itching and nausea, and lasts much longer than ketamine.","Many cough products also contain acetaminophen (liver failure), chlorpheniramine (Coricidin: seizures, heart effects) or other drugs, which add serious dangers. DXM with SSRIs or MAOIs can cause serotonin syndrome."],
["Effects begin 30 minutes to 1 hour after swallowing, often with nausea and warmth.","A drunk, giddy feeling and heavy limbs build.","The peak, 2 to 4 hours in, brings the strongest dissociation and altered thinking.","The plateau lasts several hours.","Comedown is long, over 6 hours or more.","Fatigue, low mood and unsteadiness.","A hangover-like fog often continues the next day."],
{"oral":t("30-60 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","3-6 h","6-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},{"oral":"Syrups, capsules, tablets."},
"DXM poisoning causes agitation, high temperature, fast heart rate, seizures and breathing problems; combination products add acetaminophen liver failure and antihistamine toxicity.",
"Comes as syrups, gel capsules and small red tablets (Coricidin). Many products also contain acetaminophen or antihistamines.","DXM is metabolized by CYP2D6 into dextrorphan; poor CYP2D6 metabolizers get much stronger, longer effects.",
lohi="Dissociative effects occur only far above the cough-medicine level, and combination products become poisonous from their other ingredients.",src=["NIDA: Over-the-counter Medicines DrugFacts","DailyMed: dextromethorphan products"])

X("nitrous-oxide","Nitrous oxide (laughing gas)","diss","Inhaled anesthetic gas (NMDA antagonist).","Laughing gas, whippets, nos, hippy crack, balloons; medical: Entonox (mixed with oxygen).",
"Discovered by Joseph Priestley in 1772; Humphry Davy described its euphoria in 1799, and 'laughing gas parties' followed. Horace Wells used it for dental anesthesia in 1844. Recreational use surged in the 2010s, including large flavored tanks sold in smoke shops.",
"Dental and labor pain relief and light sedation, always mixed with oxygen.","Inhaled from balloons at festivals and parties.","Not federally scheduled in the US; selling it for inhaling is illegal in many states, and the FDA warned against flavored products in 2025. UK Class C since 2023.",
["Nitrous gives a fast, short rush: tingling, a ringing or 'wah-wah' sound, laughter and dreamy detachment lasting a minute or two.","Dizziness and loss of balance cause falls. Breathing it without oxygen can cause loss of consciousness and suffocation.","Frequent or heavy use inactivates vitamin B12, which can cause numbness, weakness and difficulty walking from spinal cord damage, sometimes permanent."],
["Effects start within seconds of inhaling.","A rush of tingling and sound distortion follows.","The peak lasts seconds to a minute.","Brief plateau.","Effects fade within 2 to 5 minutes.","Brief headache or dizziness.","Normally none, but repeated heavy use causes nerve damage."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","10-20 s","20-60 s","1-2 min","2-5 min","minutes","none (repeated use: nerve damage)")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Inhaled gas."},
"Deaths come from suffocation (especially with bags or masks), falls and accidents; heavy use causes B12-related nerve damage.",
"Sold as small metal chargers or large colorful tanks; the released gas is extremely cold and can cause frostbite of lips and throat.","Nitrous blocks NMDA receptors and inactivates vitamin B12.",
lohi="More repeated inhalations raise the risk of fainting, falling and lack of oxygen, and, over weeks, nerve damage.",src=["Garakani A. et al. Neurologic, psychiatric and other medical manifestations of nitrous oxide abuse. Am J Addict 2016","FDA: warning on nitrous oxide products (2025)"])

X("propofol","Propofol","diss","Intravenous general anesthetic acting at GABA-A receptors.","Diprivan; nickname 'milk of amnesia'.",
"Developed by John Glen and colleagues at ICI, first tested clinically in 1977 and reformulated as an emulsion; FDA-approved in 1989. It became the most widely used IV anesthetic. Misuse occurs mainly among healthcare workers; it drew public attention after Michael Jackson's death in 2009.",
"Starting and maintaining anesthesia, ICU sedation, and procedural sedation, always with airway monitoring.","Rare outside healthcare; misuse by anesthesia staff has a high death rate.","Not federally scheduled in the US; prescription-only and restricted to clinical settings.",
["Propofol causes very fast, smooth loss of consciousness, often with a pleasant, dreamy waking and sometimes euphoria or disinhibition.","It stops breathing easily and drops blood pressure, so it is only used where breathing can be supported.","Outside a monitored setting it is one of the most dangerous drugs to misuse."],
["Given intravenously, unconsciousness occurs within about 30 to 60 seconds.","Breathing slows and blood pressure falls.","Deep sedation lasts minutes.","Effects last only as long as it is given.","People wake within minutes of stopping.","Mild drowsiness, sometimes euphoria.","Usually none."],
{"injected":t("30-60 s","about 1 min","1-5 min","as long as infused","5-10 min","under 1 h","none")},{"injected":"Intravenous only, in medical settings."},
"Propofol stops breathing and causes dangerously low blood pressure; outside monitoring it is frequently fatal.",
"Propofol is a white, milky emulsion in glass vials, used only in hospitals and clinics.","Propofol enhances GABA-A receptors and is very short-acting.",src=["DailyMed: Diprivan label","Wischmeyer PE. et al. A survey of propofol abuse in academic anesthesia programs. Anesth Analg 2007"])

X("etomidate","Etomidate","diss","Intravenous anesthetic acting at GABA-A receptors.","Amidate; in East Asia, etomidate-laced vapes called 'space oil' or 'zombie vapes'.",
"Introduced by Janssen in 1972 and valued in emergencies because it keeps blood pressure stable. Since about 2021, etomidate has been illegally added to e-cigarettes in China, Hong Kong, Taiwan, Singapore and elsewhere.",
"Emergency intubation and anesthesia in unstable patients.","Etomidate vapes cause jerking, unsteadiness and 'zombie-like' behavior; several Asian governments urgently controlled it.","Prescription-only in the US; controlled in China (2023), Hong Kong, Taiwan and Singapore.",
["Etomidate produces sudden sleepiness and loss of control, often with involuntary muscle jerks (myoclonus).","Repeated use suppresses the adrenal glands' production of cortisol, which can be life-threatening.","Vapes containing it look like ordinary e-cigarettes, and users often do not know what is in them."],
["IV etomidate causes unconsciousness within about a minute; vaped, effects come within seconds to minutes.","Sedation and muscle jerks.","The peak lasts minutes.","Short plateau.","Recovery within minutes to an hour.","Confusion and fatigue.","With repeated use: adrenal suppression and weakness."],
{"injected":t("30-60 s","about 1 min","1-5 min","3-10 min","under 1 h","hours","none"),"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","about 1 min","minutes","minutes","under 1 h","hours","fatigue with repeated use")},
{"injected":"Medical IV.","inhaled-smoked":"Illicit vapes."},"Risks include stopped breathing, falls, and adrenal crisis with repeated use.",
"Medical etomidate is a clear liquid in vials. Illicit etomidate vapes look like standard e-cigarette pods."+CW,"Etomidate enhances GABA-A receptors and blocks cortisol production.",src=["DailyMed: Amidate label","Hong Kong Government announcements on etomidate control (2023)"])

X("methoxetamine","Methoxetamine (MXE)","diss","Research-chemical dissociative, a ketamine analog.","MXE, mexxy.",
"Appeared online around 2010, marketed as a 'bladder-friendly ketamine', a claim not supported. Linked to deaths, it was controlled in the UK in 2012 and added to UN Schedule II in 2016.",
"None.","Sold online as a research chemical in the early 2010s.","US Schedule I (2022); UN 1971 Convention Schedule II.",
["MXE was slower and longer than ketamine, with more mood lift and stimulation; deep dissociation could arrive late.","The delayed onset led people to take more. Reported harms include agitation, high blood pressure, fast heart rate, confusion and catatonia.","Bladder damage was reported despite the marketing."],
["Onset is slower than ketamine, often 10 to 30 minutes snorted.","Gradual detachment.","Peak 1 to 2 hours.","Long plateau.","Fades over several hours.","Low mood and fatigue.","Next-day fog."],
{"intranasal":t("10-30 min","30-60 min","1-2 h","2-3 h","3-5 h","5-12 h","next day")},{"intranasal":"Snorted powder."},"Emergencies include agitation, high blood pressure, seizures and catatonia; deaths often involved other drugs.",
"White powder."+CW,"An NMDA antagonist with a longer duration than ketamine.",src=["WHO ECDD critical review: methoxetamine (2014)"])

X("rc-dissociatives","Research-chemical dissociatives (3-MeO-PCP, 2-FDCK, deschloroketamine, diphenidine and others)","diss","Designer NMDA antagonists.","3-MeO-PCP, 3-MeO-PCE, 2-fluorodeschloroketamine (2-FDCK), deschloroketamine (DCK), O-PCE, diphenidine, ephenidine.",
"After MXE was banned, new analogs of PCP and ketamine appeared from about 2012 onward, each sold until it was controlled.","None.","Sold as 'legal ketamine' or research chemicals, and sometimes sold as ketamine itself.","Many are Schedule I in the US (3-MeO-PCP since 2020) or controlled in Europe; others fall under analog laws.",
["These drugs vary widely: PCP analogs like 3-MeO-PCP are stimulating and long-lasting, with risks of mania and psychosis; ketamine analogs like 2-FDCK feel closer to ketamine.","Potency varies hugely between analogs.","Hospital reports describe agitation, high blood pressure, fast heart rate, seizures and deaths, particularly with 3-MeO-PCP."],
["Onset varies by drug, from minutes to an hour.","Detachment and stimulation build.","Peaks may last several hours.","Long plateau with PCP analogs.","Comedown can last into the next day.","Insomnia, low mood, confusion.","Lingering effects for a day or more."],
{"intranasal":t("5-30 min","15-60 min","1-3 h","2-4 h","4-12 h","12-24 h","next day"),"oral":t("15-60 min","1-2 h","2-4 h","3-6 h","6-12 h","12-24 h","next day")},
{"intranasal":"Snorted.","oral":"Swallowed."},"Severe poisonings and deaths are reported, especially with 3-MeO-PCP.","Usually unlabeled white powders."+CW,"All block NMDA receptors, with differing potencies.",
lohi="Because each analog has a different potency, an amount that feels mild with one can be overwhelming with another.",src=["Bäckberg M. et al. Intoxications of the new psychoactive substance 3-MeO-PCP. J Med Toxicol 2015"])

X("poppers","Poppers (alkyl nitrites)","inh","Volatile alkyl nitrites that widen blood vessels.","Amyl nitrite (medical), isobutyl and isopropyl nitrite; sold as 'room odorizer' or 'leather cleaner'; brands such as Rush and Locker Room.",
"Amyl nitrite was first made by Antoine Balard in 1844 and used from 1867 by Thomas Lauder Brunton for angina; crushable glass ampules that 'popped' gave the name. Recreational use spread in 1970s disco and gay club culture.",
"Historically angina; amyl nitrite was part of the old cyanide antidote kit.","Inhaled for a brief head rush and muscle relaxation, often in sexual settings.","Amyl nitrite is prescription-only in the US; Congress banned butyl nitrite in 1988 and other nitrites for inhaling in 1990, but products are sold as cleaners. The FDA warned about poppers in 2021.",
["Poppers cause a sudden head rush, warmth, flushing and a pounding heart lasting a couple of minutes, with relaxation of smooth muscle.","Headache and dizziness are common; blood pressure can fall enough to faint.","With erectile dysfunction medicines (sildenafil, tadalafil) blood pressure can drop dangerously, risking heart attack or stroke. Swallowing the liquid can be fatal (methemoglobinemia), and some users develop eye damage (poppers maculopathy)."],
["Effects start within seconds of inhaling.","A rush of warmth and flushing.","The peak lasts about a minute.","Short plateau.","Fades over 2 to 5 minutes.","Headache.","Usually none."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","10-30 s","30-90 s","1-2 min","2-5 min","minutes","none")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Vapors inhaled."},
"Dangers include fainting, dangerously low blood pressure, methemoglobinemia (blue lips, breathlessness), and death if swallowed.",
"Small brown or clear glass bottles of strong-smelling liquid. The liquid burns skin and eyes."+CW,"Nitrites release nitric oxide, relaxing blood vessels.",src=["FDA: Warning about poppers (2021)"])

X("solvents","Solvents and gases (glue, gasoline, lighter fluid, paint thinner)","inh","Volatile hydrocarbons that depress the brain like anesthetics.","Huffing, sniffing, bagging; toluene, butane, propane, gasoline.",
"Solvent sniffing was reported in the 1950s and spread among young people, especially in poor communities and among Indigenous youth in Australia and Canada, because products are cheap and legal.",
"None.","Most common in early adolescence; inhalants are among the first substances some children try (NIDA Monitoring the Future).","Products are legal, but many US states restrict sales to minors and outlaw misuse.",
["Solvents cause fast, alcohol-like intoxication: dizziness, giddiness, slurred speech and loss of inhibition, sometimes hallucinations.","Butane and other gases can trigger 'sudden sniffing death' from heart rhythm failure, even on the first use; suffocation and choking also kill.","Long-term toluene use damages the brain's white matter, causing memory and movement problems, and harms hearing, liver and kidneys."],
["Effects begin within seconds.","Dizziness and giddiness rise quickly.","The peak lasts minutes.","Users often repeat to keep effects going.","Fades within minutes to an hour.","Headache, nausea and drowsiness.","Hangover; long-term damage with repeated use."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","seconds-minutes","1-5 min","minutes","15-45 min","hours","next day")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Vapors inhaled."},
"Sudden sniffing death, suffocation, burns and choking are the main dangers; there is no safe way to inhale solvents.",
"Ordinary household products: glues, markers, fuels, correction fluid, lighter refills.","Solvents dissolve in fat and act on many brain receptors, like anesthetics.",src=["Bass M. Sudden sniffing death. JAMA 1970"])

X("aerosols","Aerosols and propellants (dusters, spray cans)","inh","Pressurized products whose propellant gases are inhaled.","Computer dusters (difluoroethane), deodorant and hair sprays, cooking sprays, whipped-cream cans.",
"Duster misuse rose in the 2000s and 2010s; difluoroethane is a frequent cause of inhalant deaths and of car crashes.","None.","Used because products are cheap, legal and in every home.","Legal products; misuse is illegal in some states.",
["Dusters cause a brief, intense dizziness and ringing in the ears, often followed by blackout.","The propellant makes the heart sensitive to adrenaline, so sudden death can occur; the very cold gas can freeze the throat.","People found unconscious holding a can are a common emergency, and drivers have crashed after using them."],
["Effects start within seconds.","A rush of dizziness.","The peak lasts seconds to a minute.","Very brief plateau.","Fades within minutes.","Headache and confusion.","Usually none, but repeated use harms organs."],
{"inhaled-smoked":t("seconds","seconds","under 1 min","1-2 min","2-10 min","hours","none")},{"inhaled-smoked":"Gas inhaled."},
"Sudden heart rhythm death, suffocation, frostbite of the airway, and crashes.","Ordinary spray cans; dusters often contain a bitter additive that does not prevent misuse.","Fluorocarbon propellants sensitize the heart to adrenaline.",src=["Avella J. et al. Fatal 1,1-difluoroethane inhalation. J Anal Toxicol 2006"])
